  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\nThe first fall cold front will move from west to east across the\nCWA this evening. Ahead of this front, expect very warm\ntemperatures and gusty west winds to develop this afternoon. At\nthis time, record high temperatures are not expected for most\nlocations today. Expect strong west winds, falling temperatures as\nthe cold front moves through. There is even the potential for a\nfew thunderstorms along the Highway 2 corridor from Browning to\nabout Havre this evening. The high wind watch was changed to a\nhigh wind warning. The strong winds should diminish by sunrise on\nSaturday.\n\nAs colder air moves into the CWA, the rain will change to snow for\nelevations generally above 5000 feet by Saturday morning. Most of\nthe precipitation will have exited the CWA, but some showers will\nlikely remain along the divide, thus do expect some minor snow\naccumulations in the mountains along the divide, including \nGlacier Park on Saturday morning.\n\nMuch of the area will then remain dry but with cooler temperature\nSaturday afternoon into Sunday. By Sunday evening, an upper level\ndisturbance will move into Southwest MT. Expect a few scattered\nshowers/thunderstorms to accompany this disturbance. Overall, most\nof this precipitation will only affect Central/Southwest MT, with\nareas over the northern portions of North Central MT likely\nremaining dry.\n\nA brisk westerly flow aloft will remain over MT for much of next\nweek. This will result in seasonable afternoon temperatures for\nmuch of next week. Additionally, another period of fairly strong\nwinds is expected Tue night into Wed over North Central MT.\n\nBy next weekend, the GFS/EC have opposite solutions, thus\nconfidence in the forecast for next weekend is low at this time.\n\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nMain concern in the short term will be the potential for strong\nwinds this evening as the cold front moves through. There is a 90\npercent chance of wind gusts over 55 mph at times in the warning \narea this evening. \n\nIt is possible a few severe thunderstorm warnings could be issued\nbetween Cut Bank and Havre this evening, as the strong winds mix\ndown with showers/thunderstorms moving through.\n\nAny impactful snowfall on Saturday morning will mostly likely be \nconfined to roadways above 6000 feet over the Northern Rockies, \nwhich will mainly just be the Going to the Sun Road, in Glacier \nNational Park. A short winter statement is possible for this \nevent. Brusda",
